  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The state of Maine has received $1.2B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense obligated $1.1B, making the Department of Defense the dominant buyer in the state. Total award activity includes 6,812 awards with an average award size of $181K. Obligations fell 68.7% year over year, reflecting a partial fiscal-year total compared with prior full-year totals. Historical annual obligations were $2.4B in FY21, $2.7B in FY22, $3.7B in FY23, $3.7B in FY24, and $3.9B in FY25. Ship Building and Repairing is the largest industry by obligations at $485M, followed by Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers at $433M. BATH IRON WORKS CORPORATION and MARTINS POINT HEALTH CARE INC are the largest vendors with $475M and $433M in obligations respectively. Other significant buyers include the Department of Labor with $28M and the Department of Veterans Affairs with $24.7M. This profile shows concentration by agency, industry, and a small number of large vendors driving the bulk of obligations in the state.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are FY2026 to date obligations in Maine?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $1.1B, concentrating the vast majority of Maine obligations. Two vendors, BATH IRON WORKS CORPORATION and MARTINS POINT HEALTH CARE INC, account for $475M and $433M respectively, which together represent a large share of the $1.2B total. Ship Building and Repairing is the largest industry at $485M, showing industry concentration as well. Award activity is 6,812 awards with an average award size of $181K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How do FY2026 to date totals compare with recent years?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 68.7% year over year to $1.2B in FY2026 to date, and current-year totals are partial. Prior full-year obligations were $3.9B in FY25, $3.7B in FY24, $3.7B in FY23, $2.7B in FY22, and $2.4B in FY21. The decline reflects the partial fiscal-year reporting compared with those full-year totals. Agencies beyond the Department of Defense include the Department of Labor at $28M and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $24.7M."
      }
    ]
  },
